// Blocked schedule: one worker, 2D outer-loop tiling via stacked
// `block=` directives on `i` and `j`.
//
// Same placement as the naive schedule (single `host` worker, all
// four kernels on it) — the only difference is the pair of `loop _
// : block=8;` directives, which tile the two outer axes into 8-wide
// chunks. Per PRD §6.3.3 `block=` is orthogonal to distribution; a
// single-worker schedule with `block=` exercises ONLY the tiling
// transform (TASK-0030's `apply_block_transforms`).
//
// What this stresses
// ------------------
// The 2D-blocking story for example 07 (PRD §9 row 7). The
// block-transform pass independently rewrites each named loop into a
// (tile-loop, intra-tile-loop) nest; stacking it on two loops gives
// a 4-level nest (i__tile, i, j__tile, j) wrapping the original `k`
// loop. The structural pinning tests (parser, sched_lower, link)
// cover this; full e2e is gated on follow-ups (see below).
//
// Why block=8 and N=16
// --------------------
// N=16 is the matrix dimension (PRD §10.1, fast CI). 16 is evenly
// divisible by 8 — so the block-transform pass's divisibility check
// (`BlockTransformError::NotDivisible`, TASK-0030 AC #4) is
// satisfied for both axes. That keeps the schedule *structurally*
// correct today and limits the open follow-ups to per-tile transfer
// hoisting (TASK-0143) rather than the broader remainder-tile
// support (TASK-0142).
//
// Why e2e is still ignored
// ------------------------
// The blocked cell is `#[ignore]`'d on the e2e gate with a TODO
// reference to TASK-0142 / TASK-0143. The naive schedule carries
// the correctness gate; the blocked schedule exists for parser /
// sched_lower / link pinning and as the foundation for the future
// distributed schedule. When TASK-0143 lands (Push/Wait hoisted to
// the tile boundary), this schedule's e2e cell flips from `ignore`
// to active.
//
// Worker placement: still single `host`. PRD §6.3.3: block= is
// orthogonal to worker placement. A multi-worker variant would add
// `partition=blocks2d` on top — that's a future distributed
// schedule's job, not the blocked one.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_a on host;
    place load_b on host;
    place save_c on host;
    place madd   on host;

    // Tile the outer i and j axes into 8x8 blocks. Each `block=`
    // directive is independent — TASK-0030 keys by loop variable
    // name. The inner k loop is NOT tiled here (a future schedule
    // could add `loop k : block=8;` for cache locality, but that's
    // orthogonal to the i/j tiling).
    loop i : block=8;
    loop j : block=8;

    // No transfer directives — single worker, no cross-worker edges.
}
